WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:08.750
It's no secret: in the wealthiest countries,
fewer and fewer people want to have children.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.770
The falling birth rate is practically a generalized
phenomenon throughout the developed world.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.759
It seems clear that it is going to become
one of the greatest challenges in the very

00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:19.500
near future: we are talking about countries
that are getting older and have fewer children.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.590
In fact, most developed countries are already
well below the replacement rate.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:28.529
That is, below the 2.1 children per woman
of childbearing age that would be needed to

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:32.020
at least keep the population stable, excluding
immigration, of course.

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.290
However, there is one specific case, a country
in which the average child per woman is not

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.500
only fewer than two children per woman, but
less than one!

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.570
We are talking about the country with the
lowest birth rate in the world.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.090
And yes, as you may have already guessed,
if only by the thumbnail and the title of

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.800
this video, I'm talking about South Korea.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:49.800
Check it out.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:55.150
Specifically, we are talking about a fertility
rate of only 0.78 children per woman in 2022.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:59.519
A level so low that we could almost say that
having children in South Korea has become

00:00:59.519 --> 00:01:00.519
an anomaly.

00:01:00.519 --> 00:01:05.260
And, take note, because in Seoul this rate
is even worse, wait for it, we are talking

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.150
about only 0.59 children per woman.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.750
Down to almost a quarter of the replacement
rate.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.240
In other words, in this case, it is not that
South Korea is going through a demographic

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:22.790
winter, it is that we are practically facing
the chronicle of an extinction foretold.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.900
Despite being one of the richest and most
advanced countries in the world, South Korea

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:30.180
is facing a colossal problem whose solution
is far from simple and which could cause a

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.740
demographic catastrophe in this country as
never seen before.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:38.650
A demographic catastrophe and, if things do
not change, perhaps an economic one as well.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:43.079
Even with the strong robotization of industry,
if things continue as they are, it is possible

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:47.830
that in a short time there will not be enough
workers to keep the economy going: its factories,

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:51.280
its stores, its schools and businesses of
all kinds.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.050
And no, we are not exaggerating.

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:57.659
We're not exaggerating at all.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:00.490
Take a look at this graph:
Do you realize what this means?

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:04.680
What you are looking at is South Korea's population
projection over the next few decades.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.170
As you can see, as of today the Land of Morning
Calm may have already reached its historical

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:10.520
maximum population.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:15.680
From now on its population will not only be
much older, but it will also begin to decrease.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.040
And don't think it will do so slowly.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.629
In the next 40 years this country will lose
more than 12 million inhabitants, that is,

00:02:21.629 --> 00:02:23.540
25% of its entire population.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:26.620
In demographic terms, that's a huge drop in
population.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.620
And that's not all.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.640
By the end of the century, if the current
trend continues, South Korea's population

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.990
is expected to shrink to less than half.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:37.519
From today’s almost 52 million inhabitants
to just over 20 million.

00:02:37.519 --> 00:02:40.769
There is no other country in the world with
such a poor outlook.

00:02:40.769 --> 00:02:44.709
We are talking about a scenario in which not
only South Koreans may end up disappearing,

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:48.340
but a lot of wealth could be destroyed in
the process meaning that those remaining will

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.890
face enormous hardship.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:57.690
Think about it, if the forecasts come true,
many of the children being born today will

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.990
see their country with half the population
when they retire, with all that implies:

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:06.269
More social spending, less savings, less consumption,
and so on and so forth.

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:10.590
And, keep in mind, we are not talking about
a vague hypothesis, but about a process that

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.019
has already begun.

00:03:12.019 --> 00:03:15.890
After years with an absolutely plummeting
birth rate, there are already many cases in

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:20.390
the country of primary schools, obstetric
and pediatric hospitals and services that

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:22.600
are having to close.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:26.239
Now, many of you may be wondering at this
point...

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:30.569
Why on earth is this happening in a country
where the economy is supposedly booming and

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:32.250
people are not lacking anything?

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.819
What exactly are the reasons why South Koreans
no longer want to have children?

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:37.909
What is the government doing to reverse this
situation?

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:38.909
Does it have a solution?

00:03:38.909 --> 00:03:42.290
Well, in this video we are going to answer
these and more questions, so...

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:46.799
Pay attention, because this is the story of
the country that is running towards its own

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:49.780
extinction.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:58.700
(THE FOUR 'NOES')

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.459
As you can imagine, a disaster like the one
we are describing is not something that just

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:03.459
happens.

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:06.409
There are reasons, and they are neither few
nor easy to explain.

00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:10.489
But here on VisualPolitik we are going to
try to tell you about it in the most summarized

00:04:10.489 --> 00:04:11.489
way possible.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:16.549
You see, the easiest thing to do would be
to blame it on low wages, hyper-competitiveness

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:20.669
in South Korea, or that women prefer to focus
on their careers rather than put them at risk

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:21.669
to have children.

00:04:21.669 --> 00:04:22.669
But...

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:26.990
No, although these factors are partly to blame,
they do not explain why South Korea is the

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.380
country with the lowest birth rate in the
world.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.919
After all, these factors apply elsewhere as
well.

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:37.259
So, what on earth has caused the problem in
South Korea to reach such a magnitude?

00:04:37.259 --> 00:04:43.729
Well, I can tell you in advance that some
things will shock you a lot.

00:04:43.729 --> 00:04:47.889
On the one hand, South Korean society is perhaps
one of the most conservative and traditional

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:49.229
of all developed countries.

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:54.080
And okay, yes, I know what you are thinking,
but [host], conservative societies usually

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.690
have an advantage when it comes to birth rates,
don't they?

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.270
Well, no, not always.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:02.699
One of the problems they have in the Land
of the Morning Calm is their extremely puritanical

00:05:02.699 --> 00:05:06.160
or traditional way of understanding motherhood
and family models.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.410
In fact, there is virtually only one family
model: the one formally composed between a

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.660
woman and a man.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.070
And then the children, of course.

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:17.350
Anything outside this framework is effectively
left out of the system, and South Korean society

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:22.410
loses a lot of opportunities to solve, or
at least mitigate, its major childlessness

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:23.410
problem.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.710
But as I know that what I have said may be
surprising and that some of you are probably

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.050
shocked, we are going to give you data and
concrete examples...

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:39.770
For example, in South Korea, only 2.9 percent
of babies born in 2021 were born out of wedlock,

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:45.569
while the OECD average is, – wait for it
– FORTY percent, 16 times higher.

00:05:45.569 --> 00:05:49.160
Nearly forty percent of children in South
Korea are simply not born.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:54.500
In other words, in South Korean society it’s
inconceivable to have children without a traditional

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:55.500
marriage.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.610
Being married is a prerequisite for having
children.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.370
And no, it is not that they are religiously
or socially very different or that they have

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:04.900
more appetite for marriage, which I already
told you they do not.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.461
It is a matter, above all a cultural one.

00:06:07.461 --> 00:06:15.880
In the case of single mothers, for example,
many face the judgment of a society in which

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.280
honor and what people will say still carry
a lot of weight.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:20.280
And what can I say?

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.300
In South Korea, having a child out of wedlock
is frowned upon.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.490
And let's not even talk about same-sex families.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.720
In general, all these things are considered
bad for children in this country.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.090
And, while children in the rest of the developed
world don’t appear to be turning out so

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:36.400
badly, in this video we are not going to debate
family structures, what we care about here

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:43.169
is the impact this rigid attitude has on birth
rates.

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:47.030
The fact is that all this cultural pressure
forces women to make a choice: to look for

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:52.830
a husband at all costs in order to have children,
or to forego motherhood and focus on themselves

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:54.139
and their professional careers.

00:06:54.139 --> 00:06:58.120
And yes, that's exactly what many women are
doing, choosing this second option.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:00.009
We'll tell you why in more detail later.

00:07:00.009 --> 00:07:03.849
But the fact is that in the end, fewer and
fewer women are willing to have children,

00:07:03.849 --> 00:07:07.510
which, logically, contributes to the fact
that the birth rate is at rock bottom.

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:10.139
But, wait a minute because it's not all bad
news.

00:07:10.139 --> 00:07:14.750
Fortunately, society is also changing and
little by little, more and more people are

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:17.580
accepting motherhood outside of traditional
marriage.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:22.530
For example, in the 10 years between 2012
and 2022, acceptance of single motherhood

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:27.410
has grown by more than 12 points, from just
over 22% to nearly 35% of respondents.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:31.669
Of course, public institutions are still slower
at making progress.

00:07:31.669 --> 00:07:37.050
For example, the South Korean Mother and Child
Health Act's definition of sub-fertility explicitly

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.720
refers to a "married couple".

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.960
That is, there is no other possibility for
receiving fertility treatment support.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.630
One way or another, the system excludes single
women from access to fertility or in vitro

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:50.310
fertilization treatments.

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:58.449
What's more, the South Korean Bioethics and
Safety Act says that anyone seeking to perform

00:07:58.449 --> 00:08:00.849
IVF treatment must have the consent of their
partner.

00:08:00.849 --> 00:08:05.039
In other words, it is taken for granted that
these treatments are exclusively for couples,

00:08:05.039 --> 00:08:10.169
which means that in South Korea it is, in
practice, impossible for a woman alone to

00:08:10.169 --> 00:08:11.350
have access to them.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:15.230
And not only that, apart from what the law
itself says, the South Korean Church is also

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.610
fervently opposed to these techniques for
unmarried women.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:21.630
Keep in mind we are talking about a country
where the relationship between politicians

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:25.830
and religious congregations is usually very
close.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:31.490
And VisualPolitik viewers, all this becomes
even more important if we take into account

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:35.760
another problem that young South Korean women
today do not want to deal with.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.820
Basically the traditional roles that still
exist in South Korean society regarding motherhood

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:45.740
and marriage.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:50.010
You could say that the professional and personal
ambitions of young women in this country have

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:53.870
changed much faster than the perception of
their role in society.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:58.380
For example, it is very, very common in South
Korea for men to leave the burden of raising

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.820
children to women, even if the woman works.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:04.320
In South Korea, children are strictly women's
business...

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:08.510
And logically, this is not easy to reconcile
with modern life and the legitimate personal

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:12.810
and professional aspirations of hundreds and
thousands, millions of women.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:16.350
It's something we've told you many times on
VisualPolitik: narratives count.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:21.630
And, no, we are not talking about "hetero-patriarchy"
or anything like that, but about social roles

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.600
that in the vast majority of the West have
already been overcome, but the same has not

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.300
happened in South Korea, nor in Asia in general.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:32.560
And then, of course, there is also the issue
of salaries, the desire for careers and extremely

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.610
expensive housing, something we have talked
about in previous videos.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:43.810
Well, the fact is that the sum of all these
reasons has led more and more young women

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:48.269
to feel that marriage and motherhood are an
unnecessary burden, or rather a problem that

00:09:48.269 --> 00:09:49.730
they have no desire to face.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:51.640
Do you want an eye-popping fact?

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:58.210
According to a 2020 survey, 42.5% of South
Koreans in their thirties were not married,

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:00.890
13 points higher than in 2010.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:04.570
(Romance booming in South Korea, but marriage
no longer the end game – The Japan Times)

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:09.750
In fact, of all households in South Korea,
as many as four out of 10 are single-person

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:16.620
households, and 2022 saw the lowest number
of marriages since 1970, with fewer than 192,000.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.890
In South Korea, young women are less and less
willing to get married and take on all that

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.680
this entails in the culture and society of
this country.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:27.140
And you have already seen that without marriage
there are no children either, or at least

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:28.589
there are far fewer of them.

00:10:28.589 --> 00:10:32.130
What’s more, divorce is also a taboo in
Korean society.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:36.060
Despite being legal, many women who divorce
face some form of discrimination, although

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:40.350
it is becoming more socially accepted thanks
to contemporary Korean TV series and films.

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:45.060
This means that a woman is acutely aware that,
if she gets married, she could have a hard

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.200
time after divorce if things go wrong.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:50.980
And this, of course, makes many of them even
more hesitant to get married nowadays.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.910
But there's more.

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:59.340
As much as this may surprise you, there is
even a women's movement known as the four

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:03.600
no's: no dating, no sex with men, no marriage
and no children.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.240
The traditional family is becoming less and
less popular among young people in this country.

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.950
And as you can see, the way things are set
up has consequences.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:14.269
But, at this point and now that you know what
the main causes of this demographic debacle

00:11:14.269 --> 00:11:17.680
are, many of you may be asking yourselves
a question: what on earth is the government

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:19.660
doing to try to solve this situation?

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:20.660
Can it do anything?

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:24.500
Well, the truth is that yes, they have done
and continue to do things.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:29.230
But you could say that... basically, it seems
as if they were desperately trying to do things

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:30.230
blindly.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:32.960
Do you want to hear what is probably the funniest
part of this whole story?

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:37.040
Well, pay close attention.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:45.120
(KILLING FLIES WITH CANNONS)

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:49.800
Let me ask you a question, what if I told
you that South Korea has squandered $200 billion

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:55.120
over the last 16 years on policies to encourage
birth rates, and yet they have achieved absolutely

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:56.370
nothing at all?

00:11:56.370 --> 00:11:59.750
$200 billion later the number of births is
at historic lows.

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:03.160
[Which, by the way, is a warning to all those
politicians who think that this matter can

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:04.720
be solved with a checkbook].

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:10.769
It turns out, the South Korean State has squandered
some $12.5 billion dollars every year on birthrate

00:12:10.769 --> 00:12:12.020
management policies.

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:16.350
This is more or less the budget that a country
like Spain allocates to defense annually.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:20.440
And as you can see, the result has been...
zilch.

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:24.420
Could it be that South Korea is the perfect
example of how the birth rate cannot be encouraged

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:25.500
with public money?

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.990
Well, maybe yes, maybe we are facing the wildest
example we can see.

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:32.600
But that’s not the worst of it, the worst
is that the government continues to opt for

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.880
this path despite admitting that it does not
work.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.410
(31 August 2022: Korea to Triple Baby Payments
After It Smashes Own Record for World's Lowest

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:41.410
Fertility Rate – Bloomberg)

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:45.269
To give you an idea, President Yoon has announced
that he would triple the baby check already

00:12:45.269 --> 00:12:48.329
in place for the first two years of a newborn's
life.

00:12:48.329 --> 00:12:53.110
This is an increase from the 300,000 won set
by former President Moon Jae-in [Pron Moon

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:58.240
Tsae-een] to one million won, which is slightly
more than $750 per month.

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:04.050
And let's not fool ourselves, the result will
probably not change anything.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:08.180
In rich countries like South Korea, having
or not having children has more to do with

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:11.990
social and cultural factors and less to do
with economic problems.

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:15.660
("If the environment is right and when people
feel they are protected and encouraged to

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.820
have children, they will have babies even
if they are told not to.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:23.149
Cash incentives alone can't really make much
progress in boosting child births."

00:13:23.149 --> 00:13:26.950
– Chung Ick-joong, social welfare professor
at Ewha Womans University)

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:31.560
Of course, the truth is that even this measure
– probably as useless as the previous one

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:35.810
– is better than other crazy ideas that
South Korean politicians have come up with

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:37.100
to deal with this problem.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.950
Some of them are so crazy that anyone would
think that they are desperate, just grasping

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:41.950
at straws.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:42.950
You don't believe us?

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:44.420
Think we're exaggerating?

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:49.050
Well, take note.

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:53.520
Some time ago, for example, a dozen legislators
put forward a proposal to flood the country

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.900
with foreign nannies paid below minimum wage.

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:59.449
In fact, the proposal included waiving the
minimum wage for foreign nannies.

00:13:59.449 --> 00:14:02.820
This is something that has already been done
in countries such as Singapore under the excuse

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:05.829
that conditions are still much better than
in their home countries.

00:14:05.829 --> 00:14:12.180
Nevertheless, it is something that raised
a lot of criticism in South Korea.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:16.410
Then there was also a proposal to exempt men
who have three or more children before the

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:19.269
age of 30 from compulsory military service.

00:14:19.269 --> 00:14:23.680
The problem is that men are the only ones
who are obliged to do military service and,

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:24.680
what can I tell you?

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:28.050
No matter how many men are willing to get
rid of the annoying military service in this

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:31.790
way, if there are no women willing to have
children, it’s a useless measure.

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:36.540
But wait a minute because now comes the really
good part.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:45.259
The two things I’m about to tell you now
have come true, this is not a joke.

00:14:45.259 --> 00:14:46.259
Check it out.

00:14:46.259 --> 00:14:50.430
(31 December 2016: South Korea pulls website
mapping women of prime age to have children.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:54.550
South Korea has closed a government website
showing the number of women aged between 15

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:59.050
and 49 - of childbearing age - by city district
and region after a public outcry.

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:02.529
[Users could look up how many women between
those ages resided in their neighbourhood

00:15:02.529 --> 00:15:03.929
for the past 10 years.

00:15:03.929 --> 00:15:06.690
– The Guardian)
It's like a kind of Farmer Wants a Wife except

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:10.509
strictly online and where the women have no
idea they’ve been selected for the show

00:15:10.509 --> 00:15:13.080
and no one even asked them if they wanted
to get married.

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.420
Not to worry though, it’s promoted by the
government...

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:16.620
Don't you think it's brilliant?....

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:17.620
Anyway.

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:20.709
Well, if that has caught your attention, you
ain't seen nothing yet.

00:15:20.709 --> 00:15:24.389
In South Korea there are local governments
that have been subsidizing the [HOST: air

00:15:24.389 --> 00:15:25.930
quotes] "purchase" of foreign wives.

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:28.199
Yes, yes, that’s what I said.

00:15:28.199 --> 00:15:32.430
(19 February 2019: South Korea's local subsidies
encourage foreign 'bride buying'; brides from

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:34.399
Uzbekistan 'most expensive'

00:15:34.399 --> 00:15:38.750
More than 35 municipal governments [...] have
implemented by-laws providing marriage subsidies

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:41.430
to local men who tie the knot with foreign
brides – The Straits Times)

00:15:41.430 --> 00:15:44.970
We are talking about a whole ‘mail order
bride’ industry that has been around for

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:47.459
a few years now in Korea.

00:15:47.459 --> 00:15:52.860
In fact, it seems to have really taken off.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:57.880
Even in Seoul itself there are numerous establishments,
businesses, where you can go, choose a woman

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:02.120
from a catalog and then, after paying a certain
fee, these companies arrange for these women

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:04.410
to fly to South Korea to marry you.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.009
Obviously, not in a forced way...

00:16:06.009 --> 00:16:07.829
But even so, it's crazy.

00:16:07.829 --> 00:16:12.250
South Korea is, along with Taiwan, the Asian
country where this practice is most common.

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:16.180
For some, this is a clear example of human
trafficking, but for others it is nothing

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:18.500
more than an intermediation like any other.

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:22.220
In fact, this type of activity has a name:
they are called marriage brokers.

00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:24.820
And take note, there are almost 400 of them
throughout the country.

00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:26.660
And I don't know, what do you want me to say?

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:31.139
Moral considerations aside, perhaps the fact
that there are public administrations subsidizing

00:16:31.139 --> 00:16:35.240
these operations with up to almost $10,000
dollars crosses all lines.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:37.570
And yes, that is exactly what some municipalities
do.

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:38.570
The result?

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:43.430
You can see it perfectly in this graph.

00:16:43.430 --> 00:16:48.110
Be that as it may, as you have seen, South
Korea has a birth rate problem so severe that

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:51.360
it may put the country itself on the road
to its own extinction.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:54.209
A problem that, far from improving, is only
getting worse.

00:16:54.209 --> 00:16:56.600
More and more women are rejecting motherhood
and marriage.

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:01.200
And that in a society and under institutions
that are still unable to accept other types

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:06.280
of motherhood or familial roles and adapt
public policies is a serious problem.

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.100
But, at this point, it's your turn.

00:17:08.100 --> 00:17:11.910
Do you think South Korea could reverse its
enormous demographic problem with other kinds

00:17:11.910 --> 00:17:12.959
of public policies?

00:17:12.959 --> 00:17:16.860
Has the old model of believing that to have
children you just need to give away more and

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:18.440
more money failed?

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:21.800
What do you think of the proposals and policies
that are being promoted in South Korea to

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:23.030
